    {
      id:'gita-verse-context',slug:'bhagavad-gita-2-47-context-and-interpretation',title:'A verse is not a slogan: reading Bhagavad Gita 2.47 in context',
      excerpt:'A familiar verse about action and its fruits deserves more than a single-line paraphrase. Begin with the chapter, the dialogue and the interpretive tradition.',
      category:'Bhagavad Gita',categorySlug:'bhagavad-gita',tags:['Bhagavad Gita','Karma yoga','Interpretation'],author:'Ananta Research Desk',authorRole:'Text & tradition',date:'2026-11-02',updatedAt:'2026-11-02',readingTime:5,
      claimType:'Traditional interpretation',status:'published',featured:true,tone:'rose',
      content:`## Locate the verse before interpreting it\nThe citation **2.47** refers to chapter 2, verse 47 in the received chapter-and-verse order of the Bhagavad Gītā. The verse is part of Kṛṣṇa's teaching to Arjuna in a longer conversation about action, discernment, duty and steadiness.\n\n## A verse within an argument\nPopular quotations often detach a verse from the surrounding discussion. That can make a complex teaching sound like a simple instruction to work without caring about outcomes. The Gītā's discussion is more nuanced: it addresses the relationship between action, intention, attachment and the discipline of the mind.\n\n## Why translations differ\nSanskrit words can carry a range of meanings, and translators make choices about grammar, philosophical vocabulary and the interpretive school they follow. Traditional commentaries may explain the same line through different theological or philosophical frameworks. These differences should be named rather than blended into a single supposedly neutral meaning.\n\n## A practical reading method\nRead the complete chapter, compare translations, consult at least one traditional commentary, and keep the Sanskrit wording visible. The Heritage site hosted by IIT Kanpur offers a canonical verse-by-verse reading path alongside thematic and commentary-oriented materials.\n\n## What this article does not claim\nThis explainer is not a definitive translation and does not settle disagreements between schools. It is a guide to citation, context and interpretive care.`,
      sources:[{title:'Śrīmad Bhagavad Gītā — Heritage, IIT Kanpur',url:'https://heritage.iitk.ac.in/SBG',kind:'Text and study resource'}],
      seoTitle:'Bhagavad Gita 2.47: Context, Translation and Interpretation',seoDescription:'Explore Bhagavad Gita 2.47 in chapter context and learn how to compare translations and traditional commentaries.'
    },
    {
      id:'exoplanet-detection',slug:'how-astronomers-find-planets-around-distant-stars',title:'How astronomers find planets they cannot see',
      excerpt:'A tiny dip in starlight can reveal a world orbiting another star. Here is how transit observations turn a faint signal into a testable candidate.',
      category:'Astronomy & Cosmos',categorySlug:'astronomy-cosmos',tags:['Exoplanets','NASA','Astronomy','Observation'],author:'Ananta Science Desk',authorRole:'Science & discovery',date:'2026-11-02',updatedAt:'2026-11-02',readingTime:7,
      claimType:'Modern scientific finding',status:'published',featured:true,tone:'mint',
      content:`## Finding a planet without seeing it directly\nMany exoplanets are identified through the effect they have on their host stars. One widely used technique is the **transit method**. When a planet passes in front of its star from our point of view, the measured brightness of the star drops by a very small amount.\n\n## From a dip to a signal\nAstronomers measure starlight over time and look for a repeated pattern. A consistent dip can indicate that an orbiting object is crossing the star's disk. The shape and timing of the light curve help researchers estimate properties such as the planet's size relative to its star and its orbital period.\n\n## A candidate is not automatically confirmed\nA signal can have other explanations, including eclipsing binary stars or instrumental effects. Researchers use follow-up observations and independent methods to test whether a candidate is truly a planet. NASA distinguishes candidate objects from confirmed exoplanets in its public science resources.\n\n## More than one method\nThe radial-velocity method looks for a star's motion caused by the gravitational pull of an orbiting planet. Direct imaging and gravitational microlensing can also contribute to the search. Different methods reveal different kinds of information and work best in different observational conditions.\n\n## How this differs from a textual cosmology\nAstronomy uses instruments, repeated observations, uncertainty estimates and models that can be tested against new data. Ancient cosmological texts belong to literary, philosophical, religious and historical contexts. They can be studied alongside modern questions, but the methods and standards of evidence are not interchangeable.`,
      sources:[{title:'How Do We Find and Characterize Exoplanets? — NASA Science',url:'https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/how-we-find-and-characterize/',kind:'Scientific reference'},{title:'What’s a transit? — NASA Science',url:'https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/whats-a-transit/',kind:'Scientific explainer'}],
      seoTitle:'How Astronomers Detect Exoplanets: Transit and Other Methods',seoDescription:'Learn how astronomers detect exoplanets by measuring starlight, radial velocity and other observational signals.'
    },
    {
      id:'yoga-heritage',slug:'yoga-as-a-living-knowledge-tradition',title:'Yoga as a living knowledge tradition',
      excerpt:'Yoga is not one frozen technique. Its practices, teaching lineages and meanings have evolved across communities and contexts.',
      category:'Yoga & Consciousness',categorySlug:'yoga-consciousness',tags:['Yoga','Intangible heritage','Practice','Tradition'],author:'Ananta Culture Desk',authorRole:'Culture & practice',date:'2026-11-02',updatedAt:'2026-11-02',readingTime:5,
      claimType:'Historical evidence',status:'published',featured:false,tone:'lav',
      content:`## A practice carried by communities\nYoga includes a range of practices and ideas, including postures, breath regulation, meditation, recitation and philosophical inquiry. The balance among these elements differs across traditions, teachers and historical settings.\n\n## A living, transmitted tradition\nThe Indian cultural heritage description emphasises the role of teachers, learning communities, manuscripts, scriptures and contemporary institutions in the transmission of yoga. That description is useful because it frames yoga as a living practice rather than a single uniform system.\n\n## Recognition and its limits\nYoga was inscribed in 2016 on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity. Intangible-heritage recognition documents cultural significance and safeguarding; it is not a medical efficacy assessment or a certification of every health claim made in yoga's name.\n\n## Reading practice and research separately\nTraditional accounts can explain aims, disciplines and philosophical commitments within a lineage. Modern clinical research asks narrower questions about defined interventions, populations, outcomes and risks. Both kinds of writing can be useful, but they answer different questions.`,
      sources:[{title:'Yoga — UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage',url:'https://ich.unesco.org/en/RL/yoga-01163',kind:'Heritage record'},{title:'Yoga — Sangeet Natak Akademi / India ICH',url:'https://indiaich-sna.in/yoga',kind:'Government cultural resource'}],
      seoTitle:'Yoga as a Living Knowledge Tradition | ANANTA',seoDescription:'Explore yoga as a diverse living tradition, its transmission and the difference between heritage records and clinical evidence.'
    },
    {
      id:'evidence-ancient-history',slug:'how-to-read-evidence-in-ancient-history',title:'How to read evidence in ancient history',
      excerpt:'Texts, inscriptions, objects and landscapes each preserve different kinds of information. Good history begins by asking what each source can support.',
      category:'Indian Civilization',categorySlug:'indian-civilization',tags:['Archaeology','Inscriptions','Historical method','Evidence'],author:'Ananta History Desk',authorRole:'History & archaeology',date:'2026-11-02',updatedAt:'2026-11-02',readingTime:6,
      claimType:'Historical evidence',status:'published',featured:false,tone:'rose',
      content:`## No single source tells the whole story\nAncient history is reconstructed from multiple kinds of evidence: archaeological remains, inscriptions, coins, manuscripts, literary works, environmental data and comparative linguistic study. Each source has its own strengths and limitations.\n\n## Material evidence and interpretation\nA building foundation can indicate construction practices and spatial organisation. A seal can preserve an image or a short inscription. A burial can reveal aspects of mortuary practice. None of these objects automatically tells us the language, identity or beliefs of every person who used the site. Those conclusions require careful context and comparison.\n\n## Texts are sources, not transparent windows\nA text has a language, genre, transmission history, audience and setting. A hymn, a royal inscription, a philosophical dialogue and a later commentary should not be read as if they were the same kind of document. Dating a text can also be complex when it has been transmitted orally or revised over time.\n\n## A useful evidence label\nANANTA uses five broad labels: primary text, traditional interpretation, historical evidence, modern scientific finding and disputed hypothesis. The labels are not a ranking of importance. They tell readers what kind of claim is being made and what kind of source should be checked.\n\n## The historian's habit\nAsk: what is directly attested, what is inferred, what remains disputed, and what new evidence could change the interpretation? The clearest writing makes those boundaries visible.`,
      sources:[{title:'Urban Heritage Atlas — Historic City of Ahmedabad, UNESCO',url:'https://whc.unesco.org/en/urban-heritage-atlas/ahmedabad/',kind:'Heritage documentation'},{title:'Archaeological Survey of India',url:'https://asi.nic.in/',kind:'Institutional reference'}],
      seoTitle:'How Historians Evaluate Ancient Evidence | ANANTA',seoDescription:'Understand how historians use archaeological, textual, epigraphic and environmental evidence to study ancient civilizations.'
    },
    {
      id:'ancient-modern-methods',slug:'ancient-wisdom-and-modern-science-compare-methods-not-headlines',title:'Ancient wisdom and modern science: compare methods, not headlines',
      excerpt:'A thoughtful bridge between knowledge traditions and scientific discovery starts with clear questions, not viral claims of identical conclusions.',
      category:'Ancient vs Modern',categorySlug:'ancient-vs-modern',tags:['Scientific method','Textual studies','Critical thinking'],author:'Ananta Editorial Desk',authorRole:'Editorial perspective',date:'2026-11-02',updatedAt:'2026-11-02',readingTime:6,
      claimType:'Disputed hypothesis',status:'published',featured:false,tone:'mint',
      content:`## Why comparisons attract us\nReaders naturally notice recurring questions across cultures and centuries: What is the universe? What is the self? How do we know what is true? Similar questions do not mean that different traditions used the same methods or reached the same conclusions.\n\n## Different ways of knowing\nA philosophical text may use reasoning, dialogue, introspection or commentary. An observational science uses instruments, measurements, models, peer criticism and reproducibility. A historical study compares manuscripts, material remains, linguistic evidence and context. These approaches can be placed in conversation without pretending that they are interchangeable.\n\n## Three checks before sharing a comparison\n- **Check the source:** is the quotation or verse accurately located and translated?\n- **Check the claim:** is the modern scientific idea being described precisely?\n- **Check the bridge:** is there evidence of a historical connection, or is this a modern analogy?\n\n## A stronger kind of curiosity\nIt is possible to value an ancient text without claiming it predicted every modern discovery. It is also possible to admire modern science without dismissing the intellectual achievements of earlier societies. The most rewarding comparisons begin by respecting both bodies of knowledge on their own terms.`,
      sources:[{title:'Rigveda — Maṇḍala 10, Sūkta 129, Vedic Heritage Portal',url:'https://vedicheritage.gov.in/hi/samhitas/rigveda/shakala-samhita/rigveda-shakala-samhita-mandal-10-sukta-129/',kind:'Primary text'},{title:'NASA Science — Exoplanet facts and detection methods',url:'https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/facts/',kind:'Scientific reference'}],
      seoTitle:'Ancient Wisdom and Modern Science: A Careful Comparison',seoDescription:'A framework for comparing ancient texts and modern scientific discoveries without false equivalence or unsupported claims.'
    },
    {
      id:'sanskrit-digital',slug:'sanskrit-in-the-digital-age',title:'Sanskrit in the digital age: preserving words with context',
      excerpt:'Digitisation can widen access to texts, but accurate preservation requires more than scanning a page: scripts, variants and provenance matter.',
      category:'Technology & AI',categorySlug:'technology-ai',tags:['Sanskrit','Digital humanities','AI','Archives'],author:'Ananta Technology Desk',authorRole:'Technology & heritage',date:'2026-11-02',updatedAt:'2026-11-02',readingTime:5,
      claimType:'Editorial analysis',status:'published',featured:false,tone:'lav',
      content:`## A digital copy is only the beginning\nA photograph of a manuscript can preserve its appearance and help researchers inspect a fragile object. To make the material searchable, projects may add catalogue metadata, diplomatic transcription, transliteration, translation and links to related witnesses. Each layer introduces editorial decisions.\n\n## Why script and context matter\nSanskrit has been written in several scripts. A digital edition should record which manuscript or printed edition it follows, preserve meaningful diacritics and distinguish a transcription from an editorial reconstruction. Normalising spelling can help search, but it should not erase the source's particular form.\n\n## Where machine learning can help\nMachine-learning systems can assist with image enhancement, handwriting recognition, classification and search. Their output still needs human review, especially when characters are damaged, ligatures are unusual or a word has multiple plausible readings.\n\n## Build for verification\nA reliable digital resource should provide provenance, version history, correction pathways and links back to the source image wherever permissions allow. The goal is not simply more text online; it is knowledge that readers can trace and check.`,
      sources:[{title:'Vedic Heritage Portal — Rigveda text resources',url:'https://vedicheritage.gov.in/',kind:'Digital text resource'},{title:'Heritage — IIT Kanpur',url:'https://heritage.iitk.ac.in/',kind:'Digital humanities resource'}],
      seoTitle:'Sanskrit Digital Preservation, Archives and AI | ANANTA',seoDescription:'How digitisation, metadata, transcription and carefully supervised AI can help preserve Sanskrit knowledge.'
    }
